.TH HTTPD 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
httpd \- serve files and CGI programs over HTTP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B httpd
.RB [ \-fv ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-h
.IR home ]
.RB [ \-p
.RI [ ip :] port ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR realm ]
.br
.B httpd \-d
.I string
.br
.B httpd \-e
.I string
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B httpd
is a small web server.
It answers HTTP requests with the files below a home directory and with
the output of CGI programs.
Each connection carries one request, and the server closes it after the
reply.
.PP
Without
.BR \-f ,
.B httpd
starts a copy of itself detached from the console, and the original
exits at once.
The server changes to the home directory, which is the directory it was
started in unless
.B \-h
or an
.B H
line in the configuration file names another.
It listens on port 80 unless
.B \-p
names another.
.PP
With
.B \-d
or
.BR \-e ,
.B httpd
does not serve.
It writes the decoded or encoded
.I string
to standard output, with no newline added, and exits.
These options are for shell scripts that handle CGI input.
.SS Requests
A request line is a method, a path that begins with a slash, and a
version that begins with
.BR HTTP/ ,
separated by single spaces.
Anything else is answered with
.BR 400 ,
except that an empty line, or none, closes the connection without a
reply.
The rest of the version is not checked, and replies are always
.BR HTTP/1.1 .
.PP
Methods are case-sensitive.
.B GET
and
.B HEAD
serve files, and both send the body.
.B POST
is accepted only for CGI programs, and a
.B POST
for a file is answered with
.BR 501 .
Any other method is answered with
.BR 400 .
.PP
The path is the text before the first
.BR ? .
It is percent-decoded, and then cleaned: repeated slashes and
.B .\&
components are removed, and a
.B ..\&
component removes the one before it.
A
.B ..\&
that would climb above the root, or a malformed escape, is answered with
.BR 400 ,
and an escaped slash or NUL is answered with
.BR 404 .
The text after the first
.B ?\&
is the query string, which is never decoded.
The cleaned path names a file below the home directory;
.B NOTES
says what that does not guarantee.
.PP
A header line of 8192 bytes or more, or one with a control character
other than a tab, is answered with
.BR 400 .
Headers that total 32768 bytes or more are answered with
.BR 413 .
A client that sends nothing for 30 seconds while the headers are read is
answered with
.BR 408 ,
and one that stops reading a reply for 60 seconds is dropped.
Headers other than those described here are ignored, except that CGI
programs receive them.
.SS Files
A path that names a directory, without a final slash, is answered with
.B 302
and a
.B Location
of the decoded path with a slash added, keeping the query string.
A path with a final slash names the index file of the directory,
.I index.html
unless an
.B I
line says otherwise.
If that file does not exist but
.I cgi\-bin/index.cgi
is executable, that program runs in its place, and the requested path is
in its
.BR REQUEST_URI ;
otherwise the answer is
.BR 404 .
.B httpd
never makes a directory listing.
.PP
A file is sent with the headers
.BR Date ,
.BR "Connection: close" ,
.BR Content\-type ,
.BR Accept\-Ranges ,
.BR Last\-Modified ,
.BR ETag ,
and
.BR Content\-Length .
Dates are in GMT.
The
.B Content\-type
comes from the suffix of the path, the text from its last dot.
Suffixes match case-sensitively, first against this built-in table and
then against the type lines of the configuration file, which win.
A file with no known suffix is sent without a
.B Content\-type
header.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
\&.txt .h .c .cc .cpp   text/plain
\&.htm .html            text/html
\&.jpg .jpeg            image/jpeg
\&.gif                  image/gif
\&.png                  image/png
\&.svg                  image/svg+xml
\&.css                  text/css
\&.js                   application/javascript
\&.wav                  audio/wav
\&.avi                  video/x\-msvideo
\&.qt .mov              video/quicktime
\&.mpe .mpeg            video/mpeg
\&.mid .midi            audio/midi
\&.mp3                  audio/mpeg
.EE
.RE
.PP
A
.B Range
header of the form
.BI bytes= first \-
or
.BI bytes= first \- last
is answered with
.B 206
and the bytes asked for, with
.I last
cut back to the end of the file.
A
.I last
of 0 also means the end of the file.
Any other form, such as a suffix range like
.BR bytes=\-500 ,
is ignored, and the whole file is sent.
.PP
An
.B If\-None\-Match
header that contains the file's
.B ETag
is answered with
.BR 304 .
The
.B ETag
is the modification time in seconds and the size, each in hexadecimal and
joined by a hyphen, in double quotes.
.B If\-Modified\-Since
is ignored.
.PP
If the
.B Accept\-Encoding
header contains
.B gzip
and a file named like the requested one with
.I .gz
added exists, it is sent instead, with
.B Content\-Encoding: gzip
and its own size, and without range support.
The
.B Content\-type
is still that of the requested name, and the uncompressed file need not
exist.
.PP
An error answer carries a short HTML page, built in or named by an
.B E
line, and a
.B 302
or
.B 304
answer has no body.
A file that cannot be opened is
.BR 404 .
A request for a file named
.IR httpd.conf ,
in any directory, is answered with
.BR 403 .
.SS CGI programs
A path that begins with
.I cgi\-bin/
runs a program.
The file must exist and be executable to Windows: it has one of the
suffixes
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
or
.BR .cmd ,
or it begins with
.B #!\&
or is a Windows program.
A file that is not executable is sent as an ordinary file by
.B GET
and
.BR HEAD .
A directory is answered with
.BR 403 ,
and a program that is not there or cannot be started with
.BR 404 .
Directories below
.I cgi\-bin
are allowed, and the path components after the program's name arrive in
.BR PATH_INFO .
.PP
A script runs under the interpreter named on its
.B #!\&
line, found as described in
.BR busybox (1),
so
.B #!/bin/sh
runs the busybox shell.
Only an interpreter in
.IR /bin ,
.IR /usr/bin ,
.IR /sbin ,
or
.I /usr/sbin
can be an applet or be found in
.BR PATH :
.B #!/usr/local/bin/perl
fails unless that file exists.
A file whose name ends in
.B .sh
and that has no
.B #!\&
line runs under
.BR sh .
A
.BI *. ext : interpreter
line in the configuration file makes any file with that suffix outside
.I cgi\-bin
a CGI program as well, run by the interpreter.
.PP
The program starts with its own directory as the current directory.
Its standard input receives the body of a
.BR POST ,
and its standard output is the reply.
Its standard error is that of the server, never the client.
It inherits the environment of
.BR httpd ,
with the variables listed under
.B ENVIRONMENT
added.
A
.B HEAD
request runs the program too, and all of its output is sent.
.PP
The program writes its headers, a blank line, and the body.
.B httpd
adds nothing to them but the status line, chosen from the first bytes of
the output.
If the output begins with
.BR Status: ,
that header becomes the status line, as in
.BR "Status: 404 Gone" .
If it begins with
.BR Location: ,
the status is
.BR 302 .
If it begins with
.BR HTTP/1.1 ,
the output is sent as it is.
Otherwise the status is
.BR 200 .
These are recognized only at the start of the output and spelled exactly
so.
The program must write its own
.B Content\-Type
and the blank line.
.SS Configuration file
At startup, and again for each connection,
.B httpd
reads
.I /etc/httpd.conf
on the system drive, or the file named by
.BR \-c .
If it cannot open
.IR /etc/httpd.conf ,
it reads
.I httpd.conf
in the home directory instead.
A
.B \-c
file that cannot be opened is an error.
Changes take effect with the next connection.
.PP
Each line is one directive.
Spaces and tabs are removed from the whole line, including inside paths
and passwords.
A
.B #
starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.
Blank lines are ignored.
A line may end in LF or CR LF.
A line longer than 159 characters is read as several lines.
A line that is not a directive is reported on standard error as
.B config error
and skipped.
.PP
The directives are:
.TP
\fBA:\fR\fIaddress\fR
Allow clients with this IPv4 address.
.I address
is one of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B *
for every address.
An
.B A:*
line has no effect.
.IP \(bu 2
Four numbers with dots, such as
.BR 10.0.0.7 ,
for one host.
.IP \(bu 2
One to three numbers, such as
.B 172.20.\&
or
.BR 10.10 ,
for every address that begins with them.
.IP \(bu 2
.IB address / bits
or
.IB address / mask
for a network, where
.I mask
has four numbers with dots.
.I address
must have no bits set outside the mask, or the line never matches.
.RE
.IP
A line whose address cannot be read denies every client, and one whose
mask cannot be read has no effect.
An IPv6 client is checked as the address 0.0.0.0, unless it is an
IPv4-mapped address, which is checked as its IPv4 address.
.TP
\fBD:\fR\fIaddress\fR
Deny clients, with the same addresses as
.BR A: .
.B D:*
denies every client that no rule matched.
A client that matches any
.B D
rule is denied even if it also matches an
.B A
rule.
Only the first character of the
.BR A ,
.BR D ,
.BR H ,
and
.B I
directives counts, and it may be lower case, so
.B allow:
and
.B deny:
work.
.TP
\fBE\fInnn\fB:\fIfile\fR
Send
.I file
as the body of every answer with status
.IR nnn ,
instead of the built-in page.
.I file
is relative to the home directory, or absolute with a drive letter.
A leading slash is the root of the current drive, not of the home
directory.
The answer carries no
.B Content\-Length
and is
.BR text/html .
A status that
.B httpd
never sends is ignored.
.TP
\fBH:\fR\fIdirectory\fR
Use
.I directory
as the home directory, in place of
.BR \-h .
Each connection starts in the new home directory and reads the file
again, so
.I directory
and a
.B \-c
file must be absolute paths, or every connection is closed without a
reply.
.TP
\fBI:\fR\fIfile\fR
Use
.I file
as the index file of directories, in place of
.IR index.html .
.TP
\fB.\fIext\fB:\fImime\-type\fR
Send files whose suffix is exactly
.BI . ext
with this
.BR Content\-type .
Later lines win over earlier ones.
.TP
\fB*.\fIext\fB:\fIinterpreter\fR
Run files with this suffix as CGI programs, by
.IR interpreter ,
which receives the file name as its argument.
The interpreter is started by exactly the name given: nothing adds
.I .exe
or searches
.BR PATH ,
so write
.I C:/tools/php/php\-cgi.exe
rather than
.BR php .
Files in
.I cgi\-bin
ignore these lines.
.TP
\fB/\fIpath\fB:\fIuser\fB:\fIpassword\fR
Require this user and password, sent with Basic authentication, for
.I path
and everything below it.
A path matches whole components and ignores case.
Several lines with the same path allow several users.
When lines with different paths match, only those with the longest path
count.
If no line matches a request's path, no password is required.
The password is plain text, compared as is, and can contain colons.
A path of
.B /
protects the whole server.
Without a valid password the answer is
.BR 401 .
.PP
The
.B H
and
.B E
lines are valid only in the first file that is read: the
.B \-c
file, or
.I /etc/httpd.conf
on the system drive.
In the fallback
.I httpd.conf
of the home directory, and in the files below, they are errors.
.PP
A file named
.I httpd.conf
in a directory below the home directory is read for each request that
reaches that directory or one below it, and its lines are merged with
the others.
An index line replaces the index file.
Type, interpreter, and password lines are added to the existing ones,
with the paths of password lines relative to that directory.
Its
.B A
and
.B D
lines replace the IP rules instead, and the client is checked against
them again; the main file's rules have already been applied, so a
subdirectory can only narrow access.
.SS Logging
Messages go to standard error, with the prefix
.BR httpd: .
Configuration errors are always reported.
With
.BR \-v ,
messages about a connection begin with the client's address and port
instead, as in
.BR "127.0.0.1:61085: response:200" .
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-fvv ,
and an argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-p8080 ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Operands are ignored.
.TP
.B \-f
Run in the foreground, and serve until the process is killed.
Without it,
.B httpd
detaches, and cannot report errors or log; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B \-v
Log to standard error.
Repeat it for more.
One
.B \-v
logs the status of each reply, two also log each request's method and
path without the query string, and three also log each connection as it
opens and closes.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR [\fIip\fR:]\fIport\fR
Listen on
.I port
(1 to 65535), on the address
.I ip
if one is given.
A port alone listens on all interfaces over IPv4 and IPv6.
A number outside that range is read as an IPv4 address instead, so
.B \-p\ 0
listens on port 80 of every IPv4 interface.
An
.I ip
is a numeric IPv4 address, or an IPv6 address in brackets, and limits the
server to that address.
Host names are not looked up.
Without a port the port is 80.
The default is port 80 on all interfaces.
.TP
.BI "\-r " realm
Name the realm in the password prompt.
The default is
.BR "Web Server Authentication" .
.TP
.BI "\-h " home
Use
.I home
as the home directory.
The default is the current directory.
A relative
.I home
is used as given, which leaves
.B SCRIPT_FILENAME
unset.
An
.B H
line in the configuration file overrides it.
.TP
.BI "\-c " file
Read the configuration from
.I file
instead of
.IR /etc/httpd.conf .
A relative
.I file
is relative to the home directory, since
.B httpd
changes to it first.
.TP
.BI "\-e " string
Write
.I string
to standard output with every byte that is not an ASCII letter or digit
replaced by
.BI &# n ;,
where
.I n
is the byte in decimal, so that the output is safe in an HTML page.
Non-ASCII characters become one such reference for each byte.
.TP
.BI "\-d " string
Write
.I string
to standard output with each
.BI % XX
escape replaced by its byte and each
.B +
by a space.
An escape that is not valid is left as it is.
.TP
.BI "\-I " fd
Internal.
.B httpd
uses it to start the process that serves one accepted connection, passing
the socket's descriptor, and, with a descriptor of 0, the process that
starts a CGI program.
The server's other options follow it.
It is not meant to be typed.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Where to look for the interpreter named on the
.B #!\&
line of a CGI script, as described in
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B BB_SYSTEMROOT
A drive, such as
.BR D: ,
to use instead of the system drive when looking for
.IR /etc/httpd.conf .
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in arguments are expanded when run from
.BR cmd.exe ,
including the strings given to
.B \-d
and
.BR \-e .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so the processes that the server starts for each
connection do not expand their arguments again.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output and messages written to a console are not converted from the
ANSI code page, and the console interprets the bytes in its own code
page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
For each CGI program,
.B httpd
adds these to the environment it inherits:
.TP
.B AUTH_TYPE
.B Basic
when
.B REMOTE_USER
is set.
.TP
.B CONTENT_LENGTH
The length of a
.BR POST 's
body, when the request gave a positive
.BR Content\-Length .
.TP
.B CONTENT_TYPE
The request's
.B Content\-Type
header, when it has one.
.TP
.B GATEWAY_INTERFACE
.BR CGI/1.1 .
.TP
\fBHTTP_\fR\fINAME\fR
Each other request header, with its name in capitals and every character
that is not a letter or digit replaced by an underscore, as in
.BR HTTP_USER_AGENT .
A Basic
.B Authorization
header, and
.BR Range ,
.BR Accept\-Encoding ,
.BR If\-None\-Match ,
and a
.BR POST 's
.BR Content\-Length ,
are not passed.
.TP
.B PATH_INFO
The path components after the program's name, with a leading slash, or
an empty string.
.TP
.B QUERY_STRING
The query string as the client sent it, or an empty string.
.TP
.B REMOTE_ADDR
The client's address.
An IPv6 address is in brackets, as is an IPv4 client of a server that
listens on both families.
.TP
.B REMOTE_USER
The user name, when the request matched a password line.
.TP
.B REQUEST_METHOD
.BR GET ,
.BR HEAD ,
or
.BR POST .
.TP
.B REQUEST_URI
The decoded and cleaned path, followed by
.B ?\&
and the query string if there was one.
.TP
.B SCRIPT_FILENAME
The home directory joined to
.BR SCRIPT_NAME ,
set only when the home directory is not a relative path.
.TP
.B SCRIPT_NAME
The path of the program, without
.BR PATH_INFO .
For the directory index program it is
.IR /cgi\-bin/index.cgi .
.TP
.B SERVER_PROTOCOL
.BR HTTP/1.1 .
.TP
.B SERVER_SOFTWARE
.B busybox httpd/
and the busybox version.
.PP
There is no
.BR SERVER_NAME ,
.BR SERVER_PORT ,
or
.BR REMOTE_PORT .
To have a
.B SERVER_NAME
in the programs, set it in the environment of
.BR httpd .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success: the string of
.B \-d
or
.B \-e
was written, or the background server was started.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, or a background server that cannot be started.
With
.BR \-f ,
also a home directory that cannot be entered, a
.B \-c
file that cannot be opened, or an address that cannot be read or bound.
.PP
The foreground server runs until it is killed, unless it cannot start
the process for a connection, when it exits with status 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Serve the directory
.I public
on the loopback interface, in the foreground, logging each request:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ httpd \-f \-vv \-p 127.0.0.1:8080 \-h public
.EE
.RE
.PP
Start a server in the background, and later stop every
.BR httpd :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ httpd \-p 127.0.0.1:8080 \-h public
$ pkill httpd
.EE
.RE
.PP
Allow only this machine and the 192.168.1 network, ask for a password
under
.IR /private ,
and add a type, in the
.I httpd.conf
of the home directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat >public/httpd.conf <<EOF
> A:127.0.0.1
> A:192.168.1.
> D:*
> /private:alice:secret
> .wasm:application/wasm
> EOF
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a CGI program, and call it while a server from the first example
is running:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir public/cgi\-bin
$ cat >public/cgi\-bin/hello.cgi <<\(aqEOF\(aq
> #!/bin/sh
> echo "Content\-Type: text/plain"
> echo
> echo "Hello, $REMOTE_ADDR"
> EOF
$ wget \-qO \- http://127.0.0.1:8080/cgi\-bin/hello.cgi
Hello, 127.0.0.1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode a value that came in a query string, and encode text for HTML:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ name=$(httpd \-d \(aqHello%2C+World%21\(aq)
$ echo "$name"
Hello, World!
$ httpd \-e \(aq<b>&</b>\(aq
&#60;b&#62;&#38;&#60;&#47;b&#62;
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from busybox
.B httpd
on Unix:
.IP \(bu 2
There are no
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-M ,
.BR \-K ,
.BR \-u ,
or
.BR \-m :
no inetd mode, connection limit, CGI time limit, change of user, or MD5
passwords.
A CGI program, and a client sending one a
.B POST
body, are never timed out.
.IP \(bu 2
Passwords are compared as plain text, so a
.B *
is an ordinary character, not a wildcard for system users or passwords.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no
.B P
reverse proxy lines and no
.B REMOTE_PORT
variable.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no reload on a signal, since every connection reads the
configuration.
.PP
The server process accepts connections and starts a new busybox process
for each, with
.BR \-I ,
which reads the configuration and serves the request.
A CGI program is started through another busybox process.
Nothing limits how many connections are served at once.
.PP
In the background, standard input, output, and error of the server are
the null device.
Nothing is logged, and a failure such as a bad address or a missing
.B \-c
file is not reported: the starting process has already exited with status
0.
Use
.B \-f
to see them.
The listening socket allows address reuse, which on Windows lets a second
server bind a port that is in use.
.PP
A reply of
.B 206
or
.B 304
has the
.B Content\-type
.BR text/html ,
whatever the file is.
The code means to pass other methods, such as
.BR PUT ,
to CGI programs, but this version answers them with
.BR 400 .
The built-in
.B 408
page says 60 seconds, but the limit is 30.
.PP
.B httpd
is not meant to face untrusted clients.
It keeps requests inside the home directory only by rejecting
.B ..\&
components between forward slashes.
A backslash, written raw or as
.BR %5C ,
is also a separator to Windows, and a path that starts with a drive
letter, as in
.IR /C:/data/file.txt ,
is absolute, so a client can read any file that the user running the
server can read.
The checks that compare the request path with a pattern see only forward
slashes: password lines, subdirectory configuration files, and the
.I cgi\-bin/
prefix, which is also case-sensitive although Windows file names are not.
A request for
.I /adm\ehome.htm
is not asked for the password that
.I /adm/home.htm
needs, and
.I /CGI\-BIN/hello.cgi
sends the source of the program.
Windows also ignores a final dot in a directory name, a final dot or
space in a file name, and a
.B ::$DATA
suffix, which names the file's contents.
So
.I /adm./home.htm
skips the password,
.I /cgi\-bin./hello.cgi
sends the source, and
.I /httpd.conf.\&
sends the configuration file, passwords included.
Passwords cross the network in the clear, and there is no TLS.
.PP
The decoded bytes of a URL path are the file name in the ANSI code page,
which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later.
On earlier versions it is the system code page, and a file whose name
has other characters cannot be served.
Windows file names are not case-sensitive, so
.I /A.TXT
finds
.IR a.txt .
The match of suffixes for
.B Content\-type
and of the
.I cgi\-bin/
prefix are case-sensitive, and the match of
.I httpd.conf
and of password paths is not.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR nc (1),
.BR pkill (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR wget (1)
